WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:19.410
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.550
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.670
Lady, it's the duel.

9
00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.990
I mean, two men are going to fight over me today.

10
00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.570
Me, a.k.a.

11
00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:32.510
Angela Martin.

12
00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:39.490
I have to say, like Andy's Prius, this episode sneaked up on me, but in a great way.

13
00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:41.310
Oh, my gosh, Hidden Gem.

14
00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:42.670
It's so good.

15
00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:44.130
It's one of my favorite episodes.

16
00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:45.570
I loved watching it.

17
00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:46.710
I loved filming it.

18
00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.350
And I'm excited to talk about it today.

19
00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:54.750
Well, it is Season 5, Episode 12, written by Jen Salata and directed by Dean Holland.

20
00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.210
Here's your summary.

21
00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:01.150
Michael takes it upon himself to expose Angela and Dwight's affair to Andy.

22
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:05.430
The two men then challenge one another to a duel in the parking lot.

23
00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:09.790
Meanwhile, Michael is summoned to corporate to speak with David Wallace.

24
00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:15.170
He thinks it's going to be bad news, but he gets some unexpected praise.

25
00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.430
Oh, those scenes.

26
00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:18.370
We'll get to it.

27
00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:19.970
But, man, those are good, too.

28
00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:21.970
Fast fact number one.

29
00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:26.510
This episode was written by the always amazing Jen Salata.

30
00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:28.230
Applause for Jen.

31
00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:29.230
Oh, Jen.

32
00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:30.370
My goodness.

33
00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:32.710
There's so many good lines in this.

34
00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:40.210
I reached out to Jen, and she sent in an audio clip explaining the inspiration behind this episode.

35
00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:41.430
Let's hear it.

36
00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:46.530
My biggest memory about writing the duel was how little I had when I was sent off to write the script.

37
00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:51.090
It was mid-season five, and it was a really busy patch for the writers, for all of us.

38
00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.350
And I remember this episode had to be written really, really quickly.

39
00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:58.190
And I knew it was about Andy finding out about Angela and Dwight's affair.

40
00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:04.190
But where we'd usually spend time as a group fleshing out the story, I went off on the script with a sentence.

41
00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:09.710
Andy runs over Dwight in his Prius, keeping it under five miles an hour so Dwight doesn't hear him coming.

42
00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:13.470
I mean, it was a good sentence, but there was a lot to fill in.

43
00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:14.490
That was it.

44
00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:15.430
She had a sentence.

45
00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.110
And she crafted this amazing story.

46
00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:18.710
Yes.

47
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:23.850
Why Andy was going to run over Dwight slowly with a Prius, she didn't know.

48
00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.530
She just knew that was going to happen in the episode.

49
00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.330
And this is what she came up with.

50
00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.190
She had to build to that moment.

51
00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.570
Because usually they would get whole outlines.

52
00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:37.310
They would have an A plot, a B plot, all of it.

53
00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:38.110
No.

54
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:39.070
Amazing.

55
00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:40.390
Jen Salata, again.

56
00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:40.970
Again.

57
00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:41.770
Applause, Jen.

58
00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:42.250
Amazing.

59
00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:47.610
Fast fact number two, this episode was directed by Dean Holland.

60
00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:51.510
Dean was our other editor alongside Dave Rogers.

61
00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:52.890
We love Dean.

62
00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:56.990
We love Dean, and he knew the show so well, much like Dave.

63
00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.850
They knew every moment.

64
00:02:59.190 --> 00:02:59.550
Oh, yeah.

65
00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:07.530
Dean told me that he had directed something for MTV, but that this was his first time directing an episode of television.

66
00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:11.990
He was so nervous that he threw up in the bathroom at Crew Call.

67
00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:13.250
Aw, Dean.

68
00:03:13.990 --> 00:03:16.190
When he texted me that, I was like, are you joking?

69
00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:17.850
He said, no, I'm being serious.

70
00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.410
I vomited in the bathroom.

71
00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:20.210
Aw.

72
00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:27.850
He said that it was really daunting because he had come from editing, and he hadn't spent a lot of time on set.

73
00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:30.790
You know, an editor's life is very isolating.

74
00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:32.310
They're just in their editing room.

75
00:03:32.410 --> 00:03:36.910
And while we would pop over and say hello, it's very different than how a set works.

76
00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:40.710
And he said he felt a little bit like a stranger in a strange land.

77
00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:41.950
Those were his words.

78
00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.750
But he also said he'd consulted a little bit with Paul Feig.

79
00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:50.410
But more than anything, he did a ton of prep work for this episode.

80
00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.010
He said he knew the script inside and out.

81
00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.170
And listen to this.

82
00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:57.190
He said it changed very little from our table read.

83
00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.550
So that was good because he had been prepping and prepping on that table read draft.

84
00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:07.590
And a lot of times, you know, after the table read, they'll come back and the whole scenes are gone and new plots.

85
00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:08.450
But no.

86
00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:08.930
Oh, yeah.

87
00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.690
He said he was really happy that not a lot changed.

88
00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.070
But I think, again, Jen Salata.

89
00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:14.590
Yeah.

90
00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:17.010
Clearly nailed this one from the beginning.

91
00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:18.510
She nailed it.

92
00:04:18.730 --> 00:04:21.950
So here's a little secret that Randy Cordray told me, Angela.

93
00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:23.510
You know, he's our line producer.

94
00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:27.510
And he's in charge of setting the schedule for the directors.

95
00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:28.370
Yeah.

96
00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:30.990
So he must have known when Dean was directing.

97
00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:36.330
He manipulated the schedule so that Dean would get this episode.

98
00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:40.310
Because when he read it, he thought Dean would be perfect to direct it.

99
00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:44.230
But oh, my gosh, Randy, look at you working it.

100
00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:44.750
Yeah.

101
00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:48.990
He said he really wanted Dean to get this one for his directorial debut.

102
00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:51.030
I mean, what a gift.

103
00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:52.210
Wow.

104
00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:58.170
Well, this was a really big episode, you know, for the whole love triangle, the Dwight, Andy, Angela.

105
00:04:58.550 --> 00:04:59.510
It was all coming to a head.

106
00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:02.150
And I was nervous, actually, about this episode.

107
00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:05.190
I just knew that I wanted to play these moments right.

108
00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:10.130
And it was a tricky kind of scenario, Jenna, because it's like Angela is worried

109
00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:11.970
about both of them and feeling guilt.

110
00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.690
But also, I don't know if she turned on.

111
00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:15.570
I don't know.

112
00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:17.030
There's a lot happening.

113
00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:21.010
And I felt very safe knowing Dean was directing me this week.

114
00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:22.630
Because, like, we knew him.

115
00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:23.490
You know what I mean?

116
00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:23.830
Yes.

117
00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:26.490
And I could go up to him and be like, Dean, did I do that right?

118
00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:27.210
What do you think?

119
00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:28.230
Should I try something else?

120
00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.290
So I felt very happy to have Dean there for the duel.

121
00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:36.590
Well, shortly after this, he went over to do Parks and Rec.

122
00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:38.470
He was the editor over there.

123
00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:38.970
Yeah.

124
00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.830
He directed a bunch of episodes there.

125
00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:45.750
But then years later, I got to be reunited with him because he was the executive producer

126
00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:49.430
and director of my show over at ABC, Splitting Up Together.

127
00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:50.310
Yes.

128
00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:53.310
And then I got to come and do a guest spot on your show.

129
00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.310
And I got to see Dean and our sound department.

130
00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:56.650
It was like a mini office reunion.

131
00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:58.090
I loved that day.

132
00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.330
It was a fun day.

133
00:05:59.450 --> 00:05:59.890
It was.

134
00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:00.790
All right.

135
00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:05.210
Fast fact number three, the history of dueling.

136
00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:07.050
What?

137
00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:08.670
What?

138
00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:08.750
What?

139
00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:10.250
Oh, no.

140
00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:12.410
What is your search history on your computer?

141
00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:13.130
Oh, no.

142
00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:16.830
First of all, I would just like to say I am not a historian.

143
00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:18.550
I'm just a lady with Google.

144
00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:21.530
You're just a delightful lady that loves to Google.

145
00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:22.290
That's right.

146
00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:29.710
But I did read about four to five what seemed like reputable websites on the subject of dueling.

147
00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:30.190
Okay.

148
00:06:30.370 --> 00:06:31.630
And here is what I have pulled together.

149
00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:32.470
Okay.

150
00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:36.250
Dueling seems to have originated in medieval Europe.

151
00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:40.290
Nobles would defend their honor in man-to-man battles.

152
00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:43.650
Well, listen, if you watch Outlander, you know that's true.

153
00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:44.710
I don't.

154
00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:45.610
But I...

155
00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:46.670
Come on, Sassanek.

156
00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:47.570
Come on.

157
00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:56.470
Well, in 1777, a group of Irishmen codified the dueling practices.

158
00:06:57.050 --> 00:07:01.630
They wrote a document of rules, and listen to what they called it.

159
00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:04.350
They called it Code Duello.

160
00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:06.890
Code Duello.

161
00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:09.790
I'm pretty sure that's the type of dessert you can get.

162
00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:11.050
I'll have the Code Duello.

163
00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:11.630
Thanks.

164
00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:15.210
I love the lemon flavor.

165
00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:24.130
It consisted of 26 very specific rules outlining all aspects of the duel, including the time

166
00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:30.830
of day during which challenges could be received, the number of shots or wounds required in order

167
00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:31.550
to win.

168
00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:36.810
I feel like Dwight would have known these 26 rules of dueling because he had the 40 rules

169
00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:39.050
all shrewd boys have to learn before they're five.

170
00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:40.070
He loves rules.

171
00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:43.010
I feel like Dwight knows about these dueling rules.

172
00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:43.990
He muSt.

173
00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:44.630
Right?

174
00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:46.230
He suggested the duel.

175
00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:47.650
Oh, I'm sure he knew.

176
00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:52.410
I'm sure that some of these rules have made their way into the shrewd life.

177
00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:54.250
Into the shrewd 40 rules?

178
00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:54.770
Yes.

179
00:07:56.050 --> 00:08:04.670
The first recorded duel in America took place in 1621 in the Massachusetts Bay Colony.

180
00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:05.150
Okay.

181
00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:10.770
And then America created their own set of dueling rules.

182
00:08:11.890 --> 00:08:17.590
They were written by South Carolina Governor John Wilson in 1838.

183
00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:23.790
So, in a typical American duel, each party has to act through a second.

184
00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:27.470
I know this from the musical Hamilton, everyone.

185
00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:28.530
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

186
00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:31.050
Oh, like you have your partner that goes with you.

187
00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:31.610
Yes.

188
00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:32.590
Like your buddy.

189
00:08:32.910 --> 00:08:33.350
That's right.

190
00:08:33.630 --> 00:08:34.110
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

191
00:08:34.270 --> 00:08:35.950
And don't you get to bring like a medic?

192
00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:36.910
Like a doctor?

193
00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:38.410
Oh, that's an outlander.

194
00:08:38.590 --> 00:08:38.970
Never mind.

195
00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:40.950
That wasn't in...

196
00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:42.870
Jamie would bring Sassanac with him.

197
00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:43.270
Okay.

198
00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:43.850
Never mind.

199
00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:44.170
Go.

200
00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:45.530
I don't know about that.

201
00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:46.230
Okay.

202
00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:48.370
I only read four to five websites.

203
00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:49.470
Okay.

204
00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:51.830
If we have a historian, perhaps they would know.

205
00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:55.370
You should watch Outlander, but don't watch it with your children around.

206
00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:55.930
Okay.

207
00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:57.150
So, listen.

208
00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:03.610
If a man felt like his honor had been insulted, he would tell his second to challenge the offender

209
00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:04.230
to a duel.

210
00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:05.690
You do not do it yourself.

211
00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:06.830
You send your second.

212
00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:13.330
The main duty of the second is to try to create a reconciliation, to avoid violence.

213
00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:15.550
Oh, so that person's like the mediator.

214
00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:16.130
Yeah.

215
00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:20.590
I mean, they're literally doing what all therapists tell us not to do, which is to triangulate

216
00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:24.750
communication in order to, you know, solve conflicts.

217
00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:25.370
But whatever.

218
00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:26.330
This is dueling.

219
00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.170
I'm the second in my family.

220
00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:32.210
I'm often the person sent in to smooth over the other stuff.

221
00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:36.210
I think a lot of people have that position in their family.

222
00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:36.850
I get it.

223
00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:44.410
But if the second goes and said, hey, my guy is offended by you and wants to duel, and

224
00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:50.850
the person says, great, we're dueling, the recipient of the duel challenge gets to pick

225
00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:54.750
the weapons and the time and the place of the duel.

226
00:09:55.370 --> 00:10:02.510
So, for example, James Shields, an Illinois attorney general, challenged future president

227
00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:04.290
Abraham Lincoln to a duel.

228
00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.330
Oh, my gosh.

229
00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:12.330
Lincoln accepted the duel and he chose the broadsword as their weapon.

230
00:10:13.190 --> 00:10:15.590
James Shields was like, what the F?

231
00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:17.090
That's insane.

232
00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:18.570
Never mind.

233
00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:20.010
Forget it.

234
00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:21.570
And he called off the duel.

235
00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:24.530
So, like, bold move by Lincoln.

236
00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:29.150
Lincoln's like, I'll show up and I'm bringing my sword to the duel.

237
00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:29.950
Do you want it?

238
00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:32.670
Lincoln was like, I'm tall and I'm a swordsman.

239
00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:33.290
Yeah.

240
00:10:33.830 --> 00:10:35.810
James Shields was like, never mind.

241
00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:36.830
You know what?

242
00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:37.130
I'm good.

243
00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:38.210
You know what?

244
00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:40.310
I thought about it and I'm okay.

245
00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:41.670
Here's one.

246
00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.030
This one really got me.

247
00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:47.190
I guess Andrew Jackson was very famous for dueling.

248
00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:47.590
Oh.

249
00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:49.050
Lots of duels, this guy.

250
00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:53.530
Experts placed his number of duels maybe as many as 100.

251
00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:54.350
100?

252
00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:56.970
Was Andrew just pissing everyone off?

253
00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:58.150
He was just pissing everyone off.

254
00:10:58.230 --> 00:10:59.190
Everyone wanted to fight him.

255
00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:05.550
His most famous duel was with Charles Dickinson, not to be confused with Charles Dickens, the

256
00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:05.790
writer.

257
00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:07.930
Oh, I was going to say, did you see my look on my face?

258
00:11:07.990 --> 00:11:08.770
I was like, wait, wait, what?

259
00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:09.210
Yeah.

260
